As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:tcas_hardware_architecture}(d), local accumulation is first performed inside the $4\times4$ MAC arrays. The following reduction stage only needs to combine four partial outputs, and therefore only requires a 4-input adder tree. Compared with the possible 64-input adder tree in a $64\times1$ GEMV design, the 4-input adder tree has a smaller fan-in and a shallower reduction depth, making it easier to pipeline and more favorable for FPGA timing closure. In other words, the $4\times4\times4$ structure does not reduce the theoretical number of MAC operations. Instead, it uses small-granularity tiles, array cascade, and cross-cycle accumulation to distribute computation and reduction pressure into a more regular and localized structure.

Therefore, under the same 64-PE scale, the $4\times4\times4$ pipelined MAC array provides better vector-output parallelism and lower top-level reduction pressure than the single-column GEMV engine. Compared with the large systolic array, it also avoids excessive global routing and data-supply pressure. This makes the $4\times4\times4$ structure a balanced design point among resource cost, pipeline regularity, and timing closure, and it is therefore selected as the shared MAC fabric in this accelerator.

\section{Non-linear Layers}
\lipsum[5-6]

